# Engineering rules

Code here is read and edited by agents as much as by people. An agent navigates by grep and
reads in chunks, so structure that helps it is a working constraint, not a style preference.
None of this happens by default — an unprompted model writes long files, wide names, and no
tests.

## Shape

- Functions 4–20 lines. Split anything longer.
- Files under 500 lines, target 200–300. One unit of meaning should fit a single read.
- One responsibility per module. Three focused files beat one file doing three things.
- Names must be searchable. If grepping a name returns mostly irrelevant hits, the name is
  wrong. Never `data`, `handler`, `process`, `Manager`, `Service`.
- Explicit types everywhere. No `any`, no untyped public functions.
- Early returns and guard clauses. Two levels of indentation, not four.
- Exception messages carry the offending value and the expected shape.
- Inject dependencies. Do not construct or import them inside the unit under test.
- Follow the framework's directory convention so paths are predictable without searching.
- Formatting is the formatter's job. Do not discuss it.
- Do not create an abstraction before it is needed.

## Comments

- Write why, never what. No captions on obvious operations.
- Record provenance where it cannot be inferred: the bug this guards against, the upstream
  issue, the business rule forcing this order, the incident that made this necessary.
- Do not strip existing provenance comments during a refactor. They are context the next
  reader needs and cannot recover from the code alone.
- Do not narrate the change being made. That belongs in the commit message.

## UI work

- A ticket with a `design:` link has its spec: match the mock's layout, states, and
  interactions exactly. Do not invent visual decisions the mock already made.
- Reimplement in the project's stack and existing components. Tokens and interaction
  semantics transfer; mock markup does not.
- Where the mock is silent, ask. Do not improvise.
- No linked design: follow `memory-bank/designSystem.md`. If direction is genuinely missing,
  offer `design-discovery`. Never block UI work on the absence of a design.

## Verification

- Every change ships with its test, in the same increment. Feature gets a test, bug fix gets
  a regression test.
- The suite runs headless from one documented command in `memory-bank/techContext.md`. No
  manual seeding, no interactive prompts, no real credentials.
- Mock external I/O with named fakes, not inline stubs.
- Run the verification commands before reporting anything as done.

## Increments

- One coherent change per commit. Never sweep unrelated work together.
- Every commit on the main branch passes verification. No "the next commit fixes it".
- Keep the verification loop fast enough that running it every time is painless.

## Refactor cadence

An agent adds code; it does not prune unless asked. So prune on schedule, not on feeling.

- Starting a feature: state the standards up front.
- After a large change, or a few hours of work: remove dead code, collapse duplication, add
  missing tests, replace magic values, split anything too large. Never change behaviour
  without tests covering it first.
- Before a release: audit for leaked secrets, dangerous permissions, destructive commands,
  path traversal, missing validation, unhandled failure paths.

Skills: `refactor-pass`, `test-pass`, `security-audit`.

## Throwing work away

- Prototype in `experiments/` before committing to a library, framework, or approach. Write
  the finding to a `decisions/` page, then delete the experiment. The document is the point,
  not the code.
- Research before implementing. Ten minutes of searching can remove days of hand-rolling.
- Getting roughly working is cheap. The last stretch is not. Say so before promising it.
